From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: forward inner flows of inline-decrypted SAs in PPE1

The outer entry of an inline SA (9999-a4) decrypts every ESP packet of
the SA in the PPE and the SOE and hands the plaintext to the CPU, which
forwards it: one TCP stream reaches 714 to 726 Mbit/s at about 50 %
router CPU. The outer entry can hand the plaintext to PSE port 4
instead, where PPE1 looks the inner tuple up like that of a packet from
the LAN. A test-only build did that (X2: outer entry 0x2988 in PPE2
SRAM, ib2 0x0007e080 = PSE port 4, NBQ 0, PORT_AG 0x3f). Every packet
that PPE1 did not forward reached the CPU on QDMA RX ring 0 with the
identity of the outer entry intact (decrypt hop flag 4, special tag
0x72a2, SW_UDF = SA index, SPORT 2), with the CPU reason of the PPE1
lookup (0x0c, 0x0d, 0x0e and, repeated while the tuple stayed unbound,
0x0f) and with the inner slot in PPE_ENTRY (0x0e10, 0x146c, 0x08c0,
0x0374: all in PPE1 SRAM). All 630167 such frames of the SA in a TCP
run were delivered, and the CPU forwarded about 700 Mbit/s that way. A
bound entry in that PPE1 slot forwards the inner flow without the CPU.

Add the module parameter soe_inline_inner, on by default. Like
soe_inline_rx it is read when an SA is added: an inline SA added while
it is set, whose byte and packet lifetimes are infinite and whose peer
is the peer of every other latched SA, is latched for it. While the
parameter is set, the outer entry of a latched SA sends the decrypted
packets to PPE1 (see Bridged flows), an inner flow of the SA that the
flowtable offloads gets a PPE1 entry for its REPLY tuple, and the
frames that PPE1 hands to the CPU are delivered like the CPU reason
0x16 frames of 9999-a4. Nothing needs to change the parameter: from
the start of the SOE the source set of bridged flows (see Bridged
flows) follows every XFRM policy change and every latched SA that is
added or deleted by itself, so the SAs that an IKE daemon installs at
boot are latched and forwarded in PPE1 without a manual step. The
parameter stays as an emergency switch. Writing 0 stops new
inner entries at once, removes every inner entry and flushes the
outer entries of the latched SAs, which are then attached again with
the CPU destination of 9999-a4 until the SAs are deleted, also if the
parameter is set again, and then drops the source set; the write fails
if an entry cannot be invalidated, and the source set then stays. An
SA added while the parameter is clear keeps the 9999-a4 path,
unchanged. Writing 1 first gives the PPE the source set and unbinds the
bridged flows that it covers; the parameter is set only then, and the
write fails, leaving it clear, if one cannot be unbound.

Gates. An inner entry is refused unless:
- G0: soe_inline_inner is set, when the flow is offered and at every
  commit;
- G1, G2: the SA was latched, which needs an inline SA (native ESP, a
  GDM device, ANTI_RPLY_EN and the SN, ICV and padding error drop bits,
  no mark, interface ID, security context, care-of address or tunnel),
  and soe_inline_inner was not cleared since the SA was added;
- G3: the state is still a valid inbound packet-offload state of this
  provider and the SA is not dead;
- G4: its byte and packet lifetimes are infinite, as neither the
  software lifetimes nor the accounting see the forwarded packets;
- G5: WAN ingress can never be looked up in PPE1, because the FOE key
  has no ingress port: PPE2 is enabled, the SA device is the WAN device
  on GDM2, GDM2 forwards every frame class to PPE2 and its loopback is
  off. G5 is checked when the outer entry is bound, which otherwise
  keeps the CPU destination of 9999-a4, when the flow is offered and at
  every commit. It covers the GDM2 WAN only: an uplink on a port that
  feeds PPE1 (a DSA user port, lan2, a Wi-Fi station) is not visible to
  the driver and counts as a LAN port;
- G6: the flow is IPv4 TCP or UDP;
- G7: the entry is an ordinary HNAPT forward entry to GDM1 (the switch)
  or GDM4 (lan2), with no HOP, TUNNEL or SOE special tag. It never
  sends plaintext to the SOE, a PPE, the WAN, the CPU or Wi-Fi, whose
  CDM4 shares PSE port 7 with the SOE.

Binding. An inner entry starts pending. airoha_ppe_foe_insert_entry()
never commits it, and no other flow, an L2 bridge subflow included,
takes the slot of its tuple there: a learned packet can come from any
port that feeds PPE1. Only a decrypted frame of the same SA that
reaches the CPU after the PPE1 lookup with CPU reason 0x0f commits it,
in the slot that the frame names, at most once per HZ/10 per slot and
only in PPE1 SRAM: WAN ingress is looked up in PPE2 SRAM and both PPEs
share the DRAM table, so an inner entry there would forward plaintext
that arrived from the Internet. It is committed from the bind word
saved when it was attached, as FLOW_CLS_STATS mirrors the state word of
its slot, an UNBIND word once the entry aged out. Frames of another SA
do not commit it; once bound, it forwards the decrypted packets of any
latched SA with its tuple, as the FOE key has no SA field, which is why
the SAs of one peer only are latched at a time. The entry keeps the
egress, L2 rewrite, QoS channel and NAT of the flow and no SOE field.
Inner entries never pass the SOE bind barrier and never quiesce the
IPsec PPE path.

Bridged flows. A LAN host that sends frames with the tuple of a future
decrypted packet from one of its MACs to another would make PPE1 bind a
bridged flow with that tuple, which forwards the plaintext to its second
MAC: an L2 bridge subflow of a MAC pair flow (tc flower, bridger) or,
after a TCP handshake or UDP in both directions, a flow of the bridge
flowtable, which this driver gets as an ordinary IPv4 or IPv6 flow. A
host of a network behind an IPsec tunnel has no MAC on this side of it,
so a genuine bridged frame never carries its address as source; only a
forged one does. While the parameter is set and an SA is latched, the
PPE therefore holds a source set: the source prefix and template of
every input, forward and socket input XFRM policy of every network
namespace that requires a tunnel-mode ESP SA, as software accepts the
plaintext of a tunnel-mode SA under such a policy only, and an SOE SA
matches an ESP tunnel template only. A bridged flow whose committed
tuple has an IPv4 or IPv6 source in the set is not committed, never
dropped: its packets stay on the CPU path until no set covers it. A flow
without an XFRM action counts as bridged when its ingress and egress
devices both belong to a bridge (the bridge, a port or a device below a
port). A routed flow is not checked: its egress follows the route of its
destination address, as that of the decrypted packet would, and the XFRM
forward policy check drops a packet from a tunnel network to the
destinations of its policy that did not come through the tunnel. The set
is read, and the bound bridged flows that it covers are unbound, when an
SOE starts while the parameter is set, after every XFRM policy change
(between the flush of the SOE flows and the end of the policy quiesce),
after the addition and the deletion of a latched SA, and before the
parameter is set again. Without a latched SA no outer entry sends
decrypted packets to PPE1, so the PPE then holds no set and every
bridged flow binds as with the parameter clear; the outer entry of an SA
latched meanwhile keeps the CPU destination until the set is installed,
which flushes it. An SOE fault drops the set for good, as the engine is
disabled and every SA is dead. The SAs of an SOE are numbered in the
order they are added. The prefix of a policy that is gone stays in the
set, with a template that admits no SA, while a latched SA that was
added before the policy went and that the template of the policy can
admit is installed: the peer can keep sending from the prefix through
such an SA, if another policy still admits it, until the SA is deleted.
The deletion of the last such SA, at the latest after its rekey, ends
it. Every other bridged flow binds as with the parameter clear. A
selector of 0.0.0.0/0 (a full tunnel) covers every bridged IPv4 flow
while an SA is latched. If the policies cannot be read while an SA is
latched (no memory, more than 256 prefixes, an ESP tunnel policy with
more than one template or a selector of another family), the set covers
every bridged IP flow, and it keeps doing so after they can be read
again until no SA latched before then is installed, as such an SA can
carry the source of a policy that came and went meanwhile. More than 256
prefixes to keep have the same effect until no SA latched before the
last of their policies went is installed, whichever template it matches,
so the set never grows beyond 256 prefixes and nothing in it outlives
the SAs it waits for. A bridged DS-Lite flow is checked by its IPv4
tuple; a 6rd flow and one of an unknown type are always covered. The
outer entry of a latched SA gets the PPE1 destination only if the
bridged flows that the current set covers were all unbound and a policy
of the set in the namespace of the SA can admit it (ESP, tunnel source,
SPI, reqid); otherwise it keeps the CPU destination of 9999-a4 until it
is attached again, as after the flush of the next policy change.

RX. A frame with the decrypt hop flag whose SW_UDF names a latched SA
and whose CPU reason is not 0x16 is the output of the PPE1 lookup. It is
identified by SW_UDF alone, as PPE_ENTRY names the inner slot, and it
is delivered with any CPU reason, as only the engine sets the hop flag
and the engine drops the packets that fail its checks. While the
parameter is set, a frame with CPU reason 0x0c to 0x0f in a PPE1 SRAM
slot carries that slot as XFRM flow ID: the flowtable can then admit
the flow from an authenticated packet and offer both directions. Every
other frame carries none, as in 9999-a4.

Teardown. SA delete, SA flush, SA forget, the flush after an XFRM
policy change or an SOE fault, a write of 0 and the flowtable destroy
remove the inner entries of the SA or of the flow; all but forget
invalidate the slot first. An entry whose slot cannot be invalidated
stays and is retried by the next flush, by the flowtable destroy and,
while the parameter is clear, by every FLOW_CLS_STATS of its flow. An
outer entry that a write of 0 cannot invalidate keeps the IPsec PPE
path disabled, as for any SOE flow. A removed entry stays in the flow
table, unlinked, until the flowtable destroys the flow, so the REPLY
direction of the flow takes the CPU path until then. The flowtable
reads the bind timestamp of a bound inner entry like that of any flow;
a pending entry carries its creation time. While an inner entry
forwards packets, FLOW_CLS_STATS also marks the SA as used, for IKE
dead peer detection and inactivity timeouts.

Trust. The decrypted packets of a latched SA get a full PPE1 lookup,
which can hit every bound PPE1 entry, and the FOE key has neither an
ingress port nor an SA field:
- A host on a port that feeds PPE1 (the LAN, Wi-Fi, or an uplink
  there) that sends packets with the tuple of a bound inner flow to the
  router MAC has them forwarded as if they came from the remote site,
  past the XFRM forward policy and the firewall. On one flat LAN
  segment that host can reach the victim directly anyway; where the
  LAN is isolated (Wi-Fi isolation, guest or VLAN zones on PPE1) it
  gains that path. Such a host could also make PPE1 bind a bridged
  flow with the tuple of a flow of a latched SA and so receive its
  plaintext; that is why bridged flows from the networks behind the
  tunnels are not offloaded while an SA is latched (see Bridged
  flows).
- The peer of a latched SA can send inner packets outside its traffic
  selectors, which software drops in the XFRM policy check, and PPE1
  forwards them when they match a bound PPE1 flow: an inner flow, a
  LAN flow to the WAN (with its NAT) or to another zone, or the
  outbound SOE entry of a flow into a tunnel, which would hand the
  packet back to the SOE (untested). Latching the SAs of one peer only
  keeps a peer out of the inner flows of the other tunnels.
WAN hosts cannot reach PPE1 (G5). The parameter documents these trust
assumptions; where they do not hold, writing 0 to it returns the
latched SAs to the CPU path.

The legacy inbound flowtable binding, airoha_ppe_foe_flow_commit_soe_in()
with its shared-entry lookup and NAT refusal, has been unreachable since
9999-a4 refused every inbound offer, and is removed.

Known limits: Inner IPv6, NAT-T SAs, Wi-Fi egress and a WAN on GDM3 or
GDM4 stay on the CPU path, as do the SAs of a second peer while those of
the first are latched, the SAs that no policy can admit yet, and, while
an SA is latched, bridged IP flows from the source set and routed ones
from it between devices that belong to bridges. Changes of socket
policies are not notified, so such a policy counts from the next policy
change. The prefix of a policy that is gone, and a set that covers every
bridged IP flow, stay as long as the SAs that they wait for, so for the
life of an SA that is never rekeyed. Policies that could not be read for
lack of memory are read again only at the next XFRM policy change or
latched SA that is added or deleted. An L2B subflow (keyed by MAC
addresses) is not checked. A flow whose REPLY tuple the flowtable keeps
deferred (a LAN-initiated flow admitted from a plaintext packet, such
as every TCP connection that a LAN host opens) is never offered for the
REPLY direction, and a flow whose entries were flushed (SA rekey or
delete, XFRM policy change, a write of 0) is not offered again; both
stay on the CPU until they end or until the flowtable resolves or tears
them down. An outer entry re-bound after it aged out keeps the
destination chosen when it was attached, so an outer entry that got the
CPU destination because no policy of the set admitted its SA yet, or
because a bridged flow could not be unbound, keeps it until it is
attached again after the next XFRM policy change. Not yet tested in
hardware: the parameter set from boot, a source set that starts with the
first latched SA and ends with the last, egress to GDM4, the end of a
kept prefix or of a set that covers every bridged IP flow once the SAs
that they wait for are deleted, and whether packets that a bound inner
entry punts to the CPU for TTL 1 or the MTU keep the decrypt hop flag;
if they do not, the driver or the XFRM policy check drops them. The
counters are kept in the SA structure; this patch adds no debugfs.

Tested on a W1700K with a test-only debugfs build, the parameter set
after boot and the PC behind the switch (GDM1): bound inner entries in
PPE1 SRAM forwarded one TCP stream at 716 to 747 Mbit/s and four streams
at 751 to 787 Mbit/s, the rate of the Internet path, at 2 to 3 % router
CPU against 45 to 57 % on the CPU path of 9999-a4. Hits from the SOE
kept the bind timestamp fresh (one entry stayed bound for 180 s), TCP
connections closed normally, and a rekey under load re-bound the flow
under the new SA within 0.6 s with a one second dip. Bridged flows of
the bridge flowtable between lan2, the switch and Wi-Fi kept binding
while the parameter was set, and a policy with two templates made the
source set cover every bridged flow.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
diff --color -urNp a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -554,6 +554,16 @@ struct airoha_flow_table_entry {
 	u32 soe_bind_hash;
 	bool soe_bind_rx_wlan;
 	struct airoha_soe_ppe_flow soe_flow;
+	/* soe_inline_inner: the SA that the REPLY tuple of an inner flow was
+	 * admitted for, the node on the PPE list of inner entries, and the
+	 * bind word of the entry, which FLOW_CLS_STATS can replace in data.
+	 * soe_inner_bridged: a flow without an XFRM action between two devices
+	 * of a bridge, see airoha_ppe_flow_is_bridged().
+	 */
+	struct airoha_soe_sa *soe_inner_sa;
+	struct list_head soe_inner_node;
+	u32 soe_inner_ib1;
+	bool soe_inner_bridged;
 
 	/* Must be last --ends in a flexible-array member. */
 	struct airoha_foe_entry data;
@@ -647,6 +657,63 @@ struct airoha_gdm_port {
 #define AIROHA_RXD4_PPE_CPU_REASON	GENMASK(20, 16)
 #define AIROHA_RXD4_FOE_ENTRY		GENMASK(15, 0)
 
+/* soe_inline_inner: the source prefix of an inbound or forward XFRM policy
+ * that requires a tunnel-mode ESP SA, in CPU order as in the FOE entry, and
+ * the template of that policy: tunnel source, SPI and reqid (0: any), and
+ * whether it admits an IPv4 ESP SA, the only kind the SOE handles. @kept: the
+ * policy is gone; its prefix stays while an SA that its template can admit
+ * and that was added before @gen, the SA generation when it went, is latched.
+ * The outer entry gate never admits an SA through a kept template.
+ */
+struct airoha_ppe_soe_src {
+	u64 net_cookie;
+	u64 gen;
+	u32 addr[4];
+	__be32 peer;
+	__be32 spi;
+	u32 reqid;
+	u8 family;
+	u8 plen;
+	bool esp4;
+	bool kept;
+};
+
+/* The prefixes of every such policy of every network namespace, and the kept
+ * ones. @all: every bridged IP flow counts as covered. @failed: the policies
+ * could not be read. Otherwise @all holds while an SA added before @all_gen
+ * is latched: the policies could not be read until then, or there were more
+ * than AIROHA_SOE_INNER_SRCS_MAX prefixes to keep.
+ */
+#define AIROHA_SOE_INNER_SRCS_MAX 256
+
+struct airoha_ppe_soe_srcs {
+	struct rcu_head rcu;
+	u64 all_gen;
+	bool all;
+	bool failed;
+	unsigned int num;
+	struct airoha_ppe_soe_src src[];
+};
+
+/* soe_inline_inner: an SA latched for it, see airoha_ppe_soe_srcs_merge(): its
+ * generation, the order in which the SAs of an SOE are added, and what an
+ * XFRM template can match of it: namespace, tunnel source, SPI and reqid.
+ */
+struct airoha_ppe_soe_sa_key {
+	u64 gen;
+	u64 net_cookie;
+	__be32 peer;
+	__be32 spi;
+	u32 reqid;
+};
+
+/* The latched SAs of an SOE, and the generation of its next SA. */
+struct airoha_ppe_soe_sas {
+	u64 gen;
+	unsigned int num;
+	struct airoha_ppe_soe_sa_key sa[];
+};
+
 struct airoha_ppe {
 	struct airoha_ppe_dev dev;
 	struct airoha_eth *eth;
@@ -660,6 +727,14 @@ struct airoha_ppe {
 	u16 *foe_check_time;
 	unsigned int soe_quiesce_count;
 	struct list_head soe_bind_pending;
+	/* Inner entries, see airoha_ppe_soe_inner_check(); flow_offload_mutex. */
+	struct list_head soe_inner;
+	/* The source set of airoha_ppe_soe_inner_set_srcs(), replaced under
+	 * flow_offload_mutex and ppe_lock, and whether the bridged flows that
+	 * it covers were unbound (flow_offload_mutex).
+	 */
+	struct airoha_ppe_soe_srcs __rcu *soe_inner_srcs;
+	bool soe_inner_srcs_ok;
 
 	struct airoha_foe_stats *foe_stats;
 	dma_addr_t foe_stats_dma;
@@ -826,9 +901,17 @@ enum airoha_soe_inline_check {
 };
 
 int airoha_ppe_soe_inline_bind(struct airoha_eth *eth, struct xfrm_state *x,
-			       u32 hash, const u8 *mac);
+			       u32 hash, const u8 *mac, bool inner);
 enum airoha_soe_inline_check
 airoha_ppe_soe_inline_check(struct airoha_eth *eth, struct airoha_soe_sa *sa);
+bool airoha_ppe_soe_inner_wan_ok(struct airoha_eth *eth,
+				 struct airoha_gdm_dev *dev);
+int airoha_ppe_soe_inner_check(struct airoha_eth *eth,
+			       struct airoha_soe_sa *sa, u32 hash);
+int airoha_ppe_soe_inner_flush_all(struct airoha_eth *eth);
+int airoha_ppe_soe_inner_set_srcs(struct airoha_eth *eth,
+				  struct airoha_ppe_soe_srcs *srcs,
+				  const struct airoha_ppe_soe_sas *sas);
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_FS
 int airoha_ppe_debugfs_init(struct airoha_ppe *ppe);
diff --color -urNp a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -44,9 +44,14 @@ static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(ppe_lock);
  * 0x16 (hit bind, force to CPU) maps to the same queue, QDMA RX ring 1.
  */
 #define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_INLINE_NBQ		1
+/* airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add(): the REPLY tuple of an inner flow of an inline
+ * SA, which PPE1 forwards after the decrypt (soe_inline_inner).
+ */
+#define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_INNER			2
 
 struct airoha_ppe_soe_info {
 	struct airoha_soe_ppe_info state;
+	struct airoha_soe_sa *inner_sa;
 	u32 tunnel_mtu;
 	bool input;
 };
@@ -752,6 +757,7 @@ airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add(struct airoha_et
 			struct airoha_ppe_soe_info *info,
 			const struct flow_action_entry *xfrm_act)
 {
+	struct airoha_soe_sa *inner_sa = NULL;
 	struct xfrm_state *x;
 	u8 dir = 0;
 	int err;
@@ -763,11 +769,16 @@ airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add(struct airoha_et
 		return xfrm_act ? -EOPNOTSUPP : 0;
 	/* An inbound SA is decrypted by its driver-owned outer entry, see
 	 * airoha_ppe_soe_inline_bind(), or by the CPU path. No inner flow
-	 * may lend its egress to the outer entry of the SA.
+	 * may lend its egress to the outer entry of the SA. With
+	 * soe_inline_inner the inner flow gets an entry of its own in PPE1,
+	 * which looks up the decrypted packets of an eligible SA.
 	 */
 	if (dir == XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_IN) {
-		airoha_soe_ppe_refuse_input(eth->soe);
-		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+		inner_sa = airoha_soe_inline_inner_sa(eth->soe, x);
+		if (!inner_sa) {
+			airoha_soe_ppe_refuse_input(eth->soe);
+			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+		}
 	}
 	if (!xfrm_act || xfrm_act->id != FLOW_ACTION_XFRM ||
 	    xfrm_act->xfrm.state != x || xfrm_act->xfrm.dir != dir ||
@@ -786,6 +797,10 @@ airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add(struct airoha_et
 		return -ENOENT;
 	if (tuple->l3proto != NFPROTO_IPV4 || x->props.family != AF_INET)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	if (inner_sa) {
+		info->inner_sa = inner_sa;
+		return AIROHA_PPE_SOE_INNER;
+	}
 
 	err = airoha_soe_ppe_flow_add(eth->soe, x, dir,
 				      &e->soe_flow, &info->state);
@@ -1440,12 +1455,104 @@ static int airoha_ppe_foe_flow_remove_en
 	return err;
 }
 
+/* Set by airoha_ppe_soe_inner_set_srcs() if the source set cannot be built. */
+static struct airoha_ppe_soe_srcs airoha_ppe_soe_srcs_failed = {
+	.all = true,
+	.failed = true,
+};
+
+static bool airoha_ppe_soe_src_match(const u32 *addr,
+				     const struct airoha_ppe_soe_src *src)
+{
+	unsigned int plen = src->plen, i;
+
+	for (i = 0; plen; i++) {
+		u32 mask = plen >= 32 ? U32_MAX : ~(U32_MAX >> plen);
+
+		if ((addr[i] ^ src->addr[i]) & mask)
+			return false;
+		plen -= min(plen, 32U);
+	}
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/* soe_inline_inner: PPE1 also looks up the decrypted packets of latched SAs,
+ * and a bridged flow with the tuple of such a packet, an L2 bridge subflow or
+ * a flow of the bridge flowtable, would forward it to the destination MAC of
+ * the bridged frames. Whether the source address of @hwe, the entry that such
+ * a flow commits, lies in a prefix of @srcs: a network behind an IPsec tunnel,
+ * whose hosts have no MAC on this side of it, so that only a forged frame
+ * carries such a source. DS-Lite keys on the IPv4 tuple too; an entry whose
+ * key this cannot read (6rd, another type) counts as covered.
+ */
+static bool
+airoha_ppe_soe_inner_covered(const struct airoha_ppe_soe_srcs *srcs,
+			     const struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe)
+{
+	const u32 *addr;
+	unsigned int i;
+	u8 family;
+
+	if (!srcs)
+		return false;
+
+	switch (FIELD_GET(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_PACKET_TYPE, hwe->ib1)) {
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_HNAPT:
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_ROUTE:
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_DSLITE:
+		addr = &hwe->ipv4.orig_tuple.src_ip;
+		family = AF_INET;
+		break;
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV6_ROUTE_3T:
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV6_ROUTE_5T:
+		addr = hwe->ipv6.src_ip;
+		family = AF_INET6;
+		break;
+	case PPE_PKT_TYPE_BRIDGE:
+		/* Keyed by MAC addresses, see the commit message. */
+		return false;
